## the deploy tier has never touched a board
# scripts/fpga-flow-deploy (1390 lines), mk/deploy.mk and the fpgahub hooks have
# been exercised against fixtures and against the CLI's own dry-run paths.
# Nothing in this repository has ever programmed a device. Every claim about
# what happens when a lease is held, a program fails, or a board is already
# busy is a claim about code that has been read, not run.
# WOULD SETTLE IT: one deploy against a real KR260 with a lease, and one against
# a board that is already leased by somebody else.

## the part/board split is a one-implementation contract
# CONTRACT.md section 1 splits the reference toolkit's single `tech` concept into
# a toolkit-side part pack and a project-side board pack. Three part packs ship;
# one of them (xck26-sfvc784-2LV-c) has ever built anything. One board pack
# exists, in one project.
# An interface with one implementation is a description of that implementation.
# The reference toolkit's tech-pack API got two things wrong before it settled,
# and it settled by acquiring a second consumer.
# WOULD SETTLE IT: a second board on a different part, built end to end by
# somebody who did not write the pack API.

## no stage script is unit-tested
# flow/vivado/{1_flist,2_package_ip,3_bd,4_synth,5_impl,6_bitstream}.tcl is 5181
# lines and needs Vivado to run, so the suite reaches it only through fixtures
# of its OUTPUT. t_measure, t_assert_stage and t_verdicts test the graders; the
# stages themselves are covered by the fact that a bitstream came out matching a
# known-good one, which is an integration result for one design on one part.
# The five flow/steps/*.tcl files are in the same position.
# WOULD SETTLE IT: the parts of each stage that do not call a tool, factored out
# and driven under tclsh the way flow_utils.tcl now is.

## `make help-hooks` and scripts/fpga-flow-hooks are named by no test
# CORRECTING MY OWN ENTRY, 2026-09-11. This used to read "doctor reports what a
# host has; hooks lists seams... which t_seams.sh already knows how to check".
# That named the wrong script. `scripts/fpga-flow-hooks` is the GIT-HOOK
# installer - core.hooksPath, the vendor-collateral pre-commit/pre-push - and
# its own header line 16 exists to stop exactly this confusion: "THESE ARE GIT
# HOOKS. THEY ARE NOT FLOW HOOKS." The thing that lists seams out of seams.txt
# is `make help-hooks` (mk/help.mk:13). I conflated the two while writing the
# entry, in a file that warns about it in capitals.
# They also need different suites. fpga-flow-hooks wants `git init` throwaway
# repos, four subcommands (install/uninstall/status/selftest), the
# refusal-on-existing-hooks path and the submodule `.git`-is-a-file case - a
# precondition class (git, and a repo to mutate) that no suite here has yet.
# `make help-hooks` needs none of that and is the cheaper of the two.
# CLOSED for `doctor` on 2026-09-11: test/shell/t_doctor.sh, 29 proofs, driven
# against a stub PATH and a sandbox MODULEPATH so host-dependence is controlled
# rather than asserted.
# CLOSED for `init` on 2026-09-11: test/shell/t_init.sh, 22 planted faults. What
# it cost is the useful part of this entry - the round trip it was supposed to
# be ("scaffold, then `make check` is clean") was WRONG. A fresh scaffold is
# deliberately incomplete and `make check` is supposed to refuse it, so the real
# assertion is that the refusal names exactly the decisions the scaffolder left
# open and nothing else. Writing the suite also turned up two defects in
# fpga-flow-init that reading it had not.
# WOULD SETTLE IT: start with `make help-hooks`, which answers from seams.txt
# and the steps directory - t_seams.sh already knows how to check that shape.

## mk/flow.mk: 1709 lines, and only its parse-time guards are tested
# t_contract.sh covers the `$(error)` guards thoroughly - 13 planted faults
# rejected - because a make conditional that stops matching FAILS OPEN and the
# build just proceeds. Nothing covers the rest of the file: the stage graph, the
# ~70-variable surface, the ordering of the `?=` chains, or any recipe.
# That gap is why this file has NOT been split into per-area fragments, though
# it is past the length a reader can hold. Everything in a makefile interacts by
# expansion order, and three separate defects in this file during its first
# fortnight were exactly that: a `:=` that snapshotted before the includes ran,
# a rule whose target expanded at parse time and resolved to the filesystem
# root, and an `$(if)` whose prose contained a comma. A split moves every line
# in the file past those same hazards with 27 assertions standing behind it, for
# no functional gain, on the one component with demonstrated bitstream parity.
# WOULD SETTLE IT, in this order: a suite that drives `make -n` over the stage
# graph and asserts the prerequisite chain and the resolved variable surface;
# THEN the split. Not the other way round.

